   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Animal health in the Pacific requires sustained resourcing to enable small-holder and commercial farmers to access the technical support they need to improve animal health, production and welfare, food safety, food and nutrition security and their livelihoods. Funding for practical work-related training is a priority for equipping a capable animal health workforce in the Pacific. 
&#x200d;
The Australian Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry (DAFF) is helping address this and trained a total of 103 Pacific animal health  staff over four activities conducted across four countries in 2024. This included 12   in Marshall Islands, 25 in Solomon Islands, 13 in Kiribati and 53 in Fiji (gender data unavailable). 
&#x200d;
Responding to the Fiji Ministry of Agriculture&#x2019;s (MoA) request DAFF trained a total of 53 animal health staff in meat inspection and food safety training at the main abattoirs in Fiji (Nasinu &amp; Vuda). This is a positive contribution to Fiji&apos;s veterinary paraprofessional capability as meat inspection training has an essential role in animal welfare, public health, safe food production and early detection of emergency animal diseases and zoonoses such as bovine tuberculosis and brucellosis which are present in cattle in Fiji.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
